NAME
       sed - stream editor: change text line by line with commands

SYNOPSIS
       sed [-n] [-E] [-i[SUFFIX]] SCRIPT [FILE...]
       sed [-n] [-E] [-i[SUFFIX]] -e SCRIPT [-e SCRIPT]... [FILE...]
       sed [-n] -f SCRIPTFILE [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       sed reads its input (the files, or standard input) one line at a
       time, applies the commands of SCRIPT to each line and prints the
       result. The files themselves are not changed unless you add -i. It is
       the usual tool for replacing text in configuration files from the
       command line or a script.

       A command can be preceded by an address that chooses the lines it
       applies to; without one it applies to every line. Quote the script
       with single quotes so the shell leaves $, * and \ alone.

   Addresses
       N            line number N
       $            the last line
       /REGEX/      lines that match the regular expression
       A,B          from address A to address B (B may be +N: N more lines)
       ADDR!        every line except those

   Commands
       s/REGEX/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS
              Substitute. Flags: g every match on the line (not only the
              first), i ignore case, p print the line if a change was made,
              N only the Nth match. In REPLACEMENT, & is the whole match
              and \1 to \9 are the groups. Another character can replace
              the slashes: s|/old/path|/new/path|.

       d      Delete the line (do not print it).

       p      Print the line (useful with -n).

       a TEXT, i TEXT, c TEXT
              Append a line after, insert a line before, or change the line
              to TEXT.

       y/ABC/XYZ/
              Replace characters one for one, like tr.

       q      Quit (after printing the current line).

       =      Print the line number.

       Several commands are separated by ; or given with several -e.

OPTIONS
       -n, --quiet
              Do not print every line automatically; print only what p asks
              for.

       -e, --expression SCRIPT
              Add SCRIPT to the commands (repeatable).

       -f, --file FILE
              Read the commands from FILE.

       -i[SUFFIX], --in-place[=SUFFIX]
              Edit the files in place. With SUFFIX, keep a backup copy
              (-i.bak keeps FILE.bak). Write the suffix right after -i.

       -E, -r, --regexp-extended
              Use extended regular expressions (+, ?, | and ( ) without
              backslashes), as grep -E does.

       -s, --separate
              Treat each file separately (line numbers and $ per file).

       -z, --null-data
              Lines end with NUL bytes instead of newlines.

EXAMPLES
       Print lines 5 to 10 of a file:

           sed -n '5,10p' /etc/passwd

       Replace every "http" with "https" in a file, keeping a backup:

           sed -i.bak 's/http:/https:/g' /etc/myapp.conf

       Turn SELinux to permissive in the config file:

           sed -i 's/^SELINUX=.*/SELINUX=permissive/' /etc/selinux/config

       Delete comment lines and empty lines, as a quick view of a config:

           sed -e '/^#/d' -e '/^$/d' /etc/chrony.conf

       Swap the first two fields of colon-separated lines:

           sed -E 's/^([^:]*):([^:]*)/\2:\1/' data.txt

       Add a line after the line that matches:

           sed -i '/^\[main\]/a gpgcheck=1' /etc/dnf/dnf.conf

NOTES
       Test a change without -i first, look at the output, then add -i. For
       one-off edits of a few lines an editor is often safer.

SEE ALSO
       grep(1), awk(1), tr(1), vim(1), regex(7)
